## 2. How the comparison was made

- Every number of A, B and the official files was turned into a key (its results.json path). The mapping from each
  official file and column to the keys is in `V\reconcile\mapping.md`.
- Tolerance: 1e-9 absolute or relative for values and interval ends; `n_finite` exactly; lists, sets and top-k names
  exactly; top-k shares within 1e-9.
- Rounded tables (`AW3_table.md`, `AW4_table.md`) were used only as a second check, never instead of the unrounded
  files.
- Keys per source: A 117,944 (107,677 numbers), B 117,946 (107,948 numbers), official 38,354 (29,023 numbers), and 921
  further keys that the reconciler rebuilt from official numbers with compare_shap's own rules (rankings, unions,
  consensus shares; a second check only).
- Keys compared: 121,664 in all (107,240 for items 1-8, 14,424 for B1). Matching in every source: 48,533, plus 157
  keys where A and B write null and the official files hold nothing (no number anywhere; listed in
  `mismatches.json`, not counted as mismatches). Mismatching: 72,974 keys, every one in `mismatches.json` with its
  group.

## 3. What matched

- **Every official number that A and B also have** agrees with both, within 1e-9, except the open choice C15 for
  families with no variable (section 4). Among the matched numbers the largest difference is 1.6e-15 (A against
  the official files: 26,082 numbers; B: 26,104 numbers). This covers:
  - item 1, the active sets J and the structural zeros (both levels, five problems);
  - item 2, every importance, share and signed mean of every model and player of J (full-sample values), every
    determined sign, every nonzero count and every top-10 list (names, order and shares);
  - item 3, the family of every variable (the same five families; the same global indices: coral 1, defo, fire and
    pm25 27, ndvi 26) and every family share (full sample);
  - item 4, every pair statistic of every pair, both levels, with its interval and finite count (the sign agreement:
    value and count only, section 5);
  - item 5, Kendall's W and the mean pairwise Spearman with their intervals, and every stability value;
  - item 6, the draw matrices (SHA-256 equal to `per_lead.json` on all five problems; same shape; same region order);
  - item 7, every main-row number the official row holds (table below);
  - item 8, the rows with pre > 0 and the top-10 names and shares of every arcsine-link model (coral's four, fire's
    treeroute).
- **The per-draw values**: `bootstrap_stats.parquet` (Spearman over J, the top-20-union Spearman, RBO p 0.9 and the
  share distance, for every pair, both levels and all 2,000 draws) equals A's and B's per-draw arrays on every draw
  (largest difference 2.8e-15). So A and B used the same draws, in the same order, as compare_shap.
- **A against B**: they agree on 94,383 numbers of items 1-8 (largest difference 8.9e-16) and on 13,294 numbers of B1
  (4.4e-16); their per-draw arrays agree on every array they both hold. They differ on 276 keys only, all of them
  open choices (C15, three groups) or the list of test-set columns each read.
- **The rounded tables**: all 3,950 rounded entries of `AW3_table.md` and `AW4_table.md` equal the unrounded official
  values written the same way; B's values equal all of them; A's equal all but 17 (the cells of families with no
  variable, where A has null, open choice C15).
- **The official files are consistent with themselves**: 320 of 320 checks passed (for example: J and the structural
  zeros equal the active flags; each share is mean_abs over its sum over J; family shares sum to 1; the main row's W
  equals per_lead.json's; `pairs.parquet` has the SHA-256 and size of its done record; the salt is
  `shap_v1|AW4|<p>|0`; the sample hash is the registered one; a variable is a global index exactly when all its
  columns are in `per_lead.json`'s `global_columns`).

