## 4. The mismatch groups and their diagnosis

Each group's keys are in `V\reconcile\mismatches.json` (key, group, the comparison statuses, and A's, B's and the
official value). "Kind": compare_bug = compare_shap.py does not follow the protocol's words; open_choice = the
protocol leaves the choice open, so both readings are acceptable (the choice compare_shap made is named, because its
outputs are the reported numbers); other = a difference of format or coverage, not of any number.

| # | group | keys | kind | changes a reported number? |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | fire's main row lacks treeroute's top 3 and own-history share | 6 | compare_bug | yes (the fire row is incomplete) |
| 2 | the sign agreement has no interval | 285 | compare_bug | no value changes; an interval is missing |
| 3 | a family with no variable: A null, B and compare_shap 0 | 55 | open_choice (C15) | no |
| 4 | the feature-level family-share distance (items 1-8) | 204 | open_choice (C15) | no |
| 5 | the feature-level family-share distance of B1 | 12 | open_choice (C15) | no |
| 6 | counts without intervals in the official files | 762 | open_choice (C08) | no |
| 7 | the main row's range without intervals | 24 | open_choice (C22) | no |
| 8 | no "median rival" named in the official row | 2 | open_choice (C23) | no |
| 9 | item 8: the official files hold rows and top-10 shares only | 2,562 | open_choice (C25) | no |
| 10 | no interval for the per-model importances (I, s, S) | 64,089 | open_choice (not a spec entry) | no |
| 11 | no interval for the family shares | 342 | open_choice (not a spec entry) | no |
| 12 | fire's undefined W and mean Spearman: NaN with 0 replicates vs null | 5 | other | no |
| 13 | the official importance file also lists the structural zeros | 3,592 | other | no |
| 14 | the official family file's mean_abs has no key | 125 | other | no |
| 15 | lists and counts no official file holds (rebuilt: all agree) | 904 | other | no |
| 16 | the test-set columns each implementer read | 5 | other | no |

**1. Fire's main row (compare_bug).** PROTOCOL.md section 8, "Main-text row (variable level)": "treeroute's top 3
variables; the consensus top 3; the own-history share of treeroute [CI] and of the median rival [CI]". The brief, item
7: "Fire's main set is treeroute alone: W and the pair statistics are undefined there, and treeroute's top 3 and
own-history share still have values." In `compare_shap.py`, `main_text_row()` writes `treeroute_top3` and
`own_history_share_treeroute` only inside `if tr and rivals:` (lines 361-386), together with the numbers that need a
rival. Fire has no same-link rival, so fire's `AW4_main_row.json` and `AW4_table.md` hold neither (the table's cells
are empty; section 14 of the protocol already describes this layout, from 2026-11-13). A and B agree: top 3 hist_mean
(0.0403), spei (0.0327), tslf_months (0.0316); own-history share 0.1358 [0.1082, 0.1647], 2,000 finite replicates. The
full-sample top 3 and share equal treeroute's values in `importance.parquet` and `families.parquet` (checked), but the
interval of the share exists in no official file. To fill fire's row, use A's (= B's) values or run a corrected
compare_shap; no other number of fire changes.

**2. The sign agreement's interval (compare_bug).** Section 8 lists the sign agreement among the "Pairwise
statistics, every pair, both levels" and says: "The importances, every statistic and every number of the main-text row
are recomputed per draw. Percentile 95% intervals, each with its number of finite replicates." Section 10 asks S3.13
for "pairs x statistics with intervals". `compare_shap.py` (lines 227-235) evaluates the sign agreement on the full
sample only and writes `ci_low` = `ci_high` = NaN and `finite_replicates` = 0, although no draw was evaluated (the 0
reads like "no draw was finite"). A and B recompute it on every draw (that draw's top-10 union and signed means; the
set of determined signs fixed by the bootstrap interval, as compare_shap also fixes it) and agree on all 102
pair-levels. The full-sample values and the counts of compared signs agree in all three sources. On 7 pair-levels no
player is determined in both models of the pair on any draw (fire, feature level: the four pairs with treeroute; ndvi:
BARDL with treeroute at the feature level, BARDL with LightGBM and with RF at the variable level); there A, B and the
official file all have no value and 0 finite replicates. On fire's variable-level ridge-treeroute pair the full-sample
value is undefined (no sign determined in both within the full-sample top-10 union), while 64 draws give a value
(A = B: [0, 1]).

**3. Families with no variable (open choice C15).** Coral has no own-history variable, defo no context or calendar
variable. Spec C15 leaves "a family with no variable in a problem" open. compare_shap gives such a family a zero row
sum and the share 0 (lines 268-274), and coral's main row gets 0.00 [0.00, 0.00] with 2,000 finite replicates; B does
the same; A writes null with its reason. The reported numbers are compare_shap's (0). The B1 problems have no empty
family, so no B1 number is touched. (For the coral and defo interval ends of these shares the official file also has no
interval, as in group 11.)

**4. The feature-level family-share distance (open choice C15).** Spec C15 leaves open "whether the feature-level
pairs carry a family-share distance, and from which phi". Families are sums of variable-level phi (section 8). A
writes none at the feature level; B repeats the variable-level number (its X03); compare_shap computes the family
distance at the variable level only (lines 281-295), which is A's choice. The variable-level family distance agrees in
all three sources.

**5. The same for B1 (open choice C15).** Section 10: where section 8 leaves a choice open, the B1 numbers "make the
main comparison's choice (compare_shap.py), as the independent re-computation establishes it". compare_shap has no
feature-level family distance, so A's B1 choice is the one to report. B's extra number equals its own variable-level
B1 family distance, which equals A's (fire 0.8114, pm25 0.7038, ndvi 0.4904), so nothing needs recomputing.

**6. Counts without intervals (open choice C08).** Section 10: "or whether a count gets an interval". A and B give the
nonzero counts, the number of compared signs and the number of agreeing signs an interval (and agree); the official
files hold the full-sample counts only (`per_lead.json` `nonzero_players`; `pairs.parquet` `signs_compared`; the
number of agreeing signs is not written, but follows from share x count and agrees). compare_shap's choice: no
interval for a count. Also C09: compare_shap counts nonzero players over J (line 333), A and B over each model's own
columns; the counts are equal for every model, level and problem. For B1 this means: by section 10's rule the B1
counts (`n_nonzero`, `n_nonzero_before_merge`, `sign_n_determined`, `sign_n_agree`) are to be reported without
intervals; their values stand.

**7. The range of the main row (open choice C22).** compare_shap reports the full-sample minimum and maximum over the
rivals (lines 379-380); section 8's row puts "[CI]" on the median only ("the median of the top-20-union Spearman [CI]
and the range"). A and B add an interval to each end and agree. The values agree.

**8. "The median rival" (open choice C23).** compare_shap takes the median of the rivals' own-history shares (numpy
median, line 385) and names no rival. A and B do the same and also name the rival whose share is that median when the
number of rivals is odd (defo and pm25: rival_lgbm). The median's value and interval agree in all three sources.

**9. Item 8 (open choice C25).** compare_shap (lines 302-315) reports, per arcsine-link model, the number of rows with
pre > 0 and the top 10 with shares, on the full sample, with shares taken over every variable of the problem. A and B
also report I, S, intervals, the nonzero count, the ranking and the regions used, with shares over the level's J. The
numbers the official files hold (coral 983, 995, 1,000 and 973 rows; fire 226 rows; every top-10 name and share)
equal A's and B's: the two share denominators differ only by the structural zeros, which are exactly 0 on those rows.

**10 and 11. No interval for the importances and family shares (open choice, not one of the spec's entries).** A and
B give every player's I, share and signed mean, and every family share, a percentile interval with its finite count,
and they agree on all of them. compare_shap recomputes the importances on every draw (lines 201-208), uses them for the
statistics and for the determined signs, and writes only full-sample values (`importance.parquet`: lines 245-248;
`families.parquet`: lines 264-275). Section 8 says the importances are "recomputed per draw" (they are) but not that
each importance is reported with an interval, and section 10 lists for S3.13 "pairs x statistics with intervals ...
each model's top-10 with shares; family shares". So both readings are acceptable; compare_shap's choice is no
interval, and no reported number changes. If S3.13 should show intervals for the top-10 shares or family shares, A's
(= B's) values exist.

**12. Fire's undefined W (other).** With one model in the main set, compare_shap evaluates W and the mean pairwise
Spearman on every draw and finds none finite (value NaN, interval NaN, 0 finite replicates). A and B write them as
undefined by definition (null, with no replicate count). Same meaning, different notation.

**13. Structural zeros in the official importance file (other).** `importance.parquet` has a row for every player and
model, J or not; the spec asks A and B for the players of J only. The 898 extra rows (3,592 numbers) are all exactly 0
(mean |phi|, share and signed mean) with no determined sign.

**14. The family mean |phi| (other).** `families.parquet`'s `mean_abs` (the numerator of each family share) has no
results.json key; the shares built from it agree.

**15. Lists and counts no official file holds (other).** Each model's ranking over J, the top-20 and top-10 unions and
the determined-in-both sets of each pair, `both_in_main_set`, the number of agreeing signs, W's m and n, the main
row's rivals and consensus shares, the consensus top-3 shares, the region order and the list of global indices are not
written by compare_shap. Rebuilt from the official numbers with compare_shap's own rules (`mapping.md`), all 904
equal A's and B's. (The 5 item-8 share sets are counted in group 9.)

**16. Test-set columns (other).** The list of test-set columns each implementer read (an I04 record, not a number)
differs: A also read the context columns, B the column `issue`. Both read the same records (the rivals' lead-0 test
export) and found the same global indices, which equal the official family map.

## 5. compare_shap.py deviations from the protocol (findings for the owner)

1. **Fire's main-text row omits treeroute's top 3 variables and its own-history share [CI]** (section 8, main-text
   row; brief item 7). Cause: `main_text_row()` writes them only when there is a same-link rival (compare_shap.py
   lines 361-386). Values to use (A = B): hist_mean 0.0403, spei 0.0327, tslf_months 0.0316; own-history share 0.1358
   [0.1082, 0.1647] (2,000 finite replicates).
2. **The sign agreement is not recomputed on the draws** (section 8: "every statistic ... recomputed per draw";
   section 10: "pairs x statistics with intervals"). Cause: compare_shap.py lines 227-235 set its interval to NaN and
   its finite count to 0 without evaluating any draw. Its full-sample values are right; A's (= B's) intervals are
   available for all 102 pair-levels.

A smaller point, with no effect here: compare_shap orders item 8's top 10 by `np.argsort(-Iz)`, which breaks ties
arbitrarily, not by name (section 8's tie rule). No tie occurred: every name and its order equal A's and B's.

No file outside `V\reconcile\` was changed; the official outputs stay as they are.

## 6. Treeroute's B1 sensitivity (item 9; A against B, no official output)

**Status.** Coral: no `treeroute_B1\` folder (section 5: "Not defined for coral"), no B1 numbers. Defo, fire, pm25 and
ndvi: the folder holds `rows.parquet.done.json` and no `FAILED.json`, so each is a finished piece. Their `meta.json`:
`pass` true, all four checks (S-ADD, S-EVAL, S-RESID, S-ZERO) true, 1,000 rows, background "B1", the registered
sample hash (defo 9989bd58, fire 1edbc3d1, pm25 ccb17021, ndvi f2bb2a79; `SAMPLE_REGISTER.txt`), trees used 4,200,
4,000, 12,700 and 13,300, and merged duplicates none on defo, fire and pm25 and the same five context columns as the
main run on ndvi. A and B copied these fields exactly as the reconciler reads them.

**Defo's identity check (first).**

| number | A | B | reconciler (own recomputation) |
|---|---|---|---|
| largest abs. difference over every row and `phi__` column | 0.0 | 0.0 | 0.0 (309,000 values) |
| the same over the `phiv__` columns | 0.0 | 0.0 | 0.0 (121,000 values) |
| same rows / players / variables | yes / yes / yes | yes / yes / yes | yes / yes / yes |
| rows, players, variables (main = B1) | 1,000, 309, 121 | 1,000, 309, 121 | 1,000, 309, 121 |
| as expected (0 and the same) | yes | yes | yes |
| background pairs (log = meta) | 16,000 | 16,000 | 16,000 |
| total seconds (log = done record) | 465.9 | 465.9 | 465.9 |
| tree-SHAP seconds (log = meta) | 464.3 | 464.3 | 464.3 |
| log lines (set-up, done) | 24, 30 | 24, 30 | 24, 30 |

So the check held, and the fire, pm25 and ndvi B1 numbers were computed (by both implementers).

**Nonzero counts against meta.json** (A = B):

