Numbers

4. Section 6, the B1 pair table: four cells were rounded twice (to four decimals, then to three) and are wrong in the
   third decimal. Fire, feature level, Kendall tau-b: 0.721 [0.640, 0.752], not 0.722. Fire, variable level,
   top-20-union Spearman: 0.391 [0.246, 0.499], not [0.246, 0.500]. NDVI, variable level, Spearman over J: 0.844
   [0.833, 0.851], not 0.845. NDVI, variable level, Kendall tau-b: 0.774 [0.757, 0.788], not [0.757, 0.789]. The
   reconciler's returned `b1_summary` carries the same digits. A6 rebuilds every B1 table from `results.json` with
   one rounding step; tables for the paper are to be built the same way.
5. Section 3: the final run compared 3,953 rounded table entries, not 3,950 (coral 1,114, defo 790, fire 754, pm25
   505, ndvi 790); B matches all 3,953 and A 3,936 (all but 17, the C15 cells).
6. Section 6, "They agree on all 14,412 B1 keys but 12": they agree on 14,412 of the 14,424 B1 keys; the other 12
   are B's feature-level family-share distance.
7. Section 3, "Every interval above has 2,000 finite replicates in all three sources": not for coral's own-history
   cells (A has null; the official files and B have 2,000) and not for fire's treeroute cells (no official value; A
   and B have 2,000).
8. "In short" (first bullet) and section 3 (first bullet): the official numbers that A and B also hold agree with
   them except in three groups, not only in the open choice C15: also the sign-agreement intervals (group 2, a
   compare_bug: the official files write NaN with 0 finite replicates) and fire's undefined W and mean pairwise
   Spearman (group 12: NaN with 0 finite replicates, against A's and B's null).

Diagnoses

9. Group 2 (the sign agreement's interval) does touch what S3.13 prints. Section 10 asks S3.13 for "pairs x
   statistics with intervals", and the official files hold no sign-agreement interval. Their `finite_replicates` = 0
   is right only on the 7 pair-levels where no sign is determined in both models on any draw; on the other 95 no
   draw was evaluated, so that 0 must not be printed. The intervals that exist are A's (= B's); three of fire's rest
   on few finite draws (A5, point 1).
10. Group 3 (a family with no variable): section 8's rule (a family's attribution is the sum of its variables' phi,
    and an empty sum is 0) already gives compare_shap's 0, so the group is the rule's result more than an open
    choice. The reconcile skeptic's fix is to keep the 0 and label it. The first version of this addendum proposed
    printing words in place of the 0; that was the lead's addition, and it is withdrawn (A5, point 4).
11. Group 8 ("the median rival"): with an odd number of rivals, A and B name the median rival only when the shares
    are defined and not tied: rival_lgbm for defo and pm25, none for coral (no own-history variable; all three
    shares are 0). NDVI has four rivals, so its value 0.2560 [0.2357, 0.2757] is the median of four shares (the mean
    of the two middle ones), and no rival has it (A5, point 4).
12. Groups 4 and 5, and section 6 (b) (the feature-level family-share distance): section 8 lists the family-share
    distance among the statistics of "every pair, both levels", and section 10 asks for the B1 pair's distance
    between the two runs' family shares at both levels, so the words favour showing it at both levels (B's reading).
    Under section 8's definition the families are sums of variable-level phi, so the feature-level value is the
    variable-level value repeated; no separate feature-level family share is defined. Section 6 (b), "B's B1 value is
    dropped", is therefore not settled: it is for the owner (A5, point 2).
13. Group 16 (test-set columns): A's extra columns are the context columns and the own-history `x__hist_*` columns
    (defo: A lists 310 columns and B 306; only in A: `x__hist_last_1` to `x__hist_last_3`, `x__hist_mean_5` and
    `x__hist_mean_all`; only in B: `issue`). That both read the same records is checked in A3.
14. Section 6, "Only `a20_b1.py` opens `treeroute_B1\`" and "Only `b_b1.py` and `b_crosscheck_b1.py` open
    `treeroute_B1\`": besides these, the inspection scripts `a04_checks.py` (A) and `b_inspect3.py` (B) open
    `treeroute_B1` files (key and flag columns, `meta.json`, the rows schema). They print only and write nothing, so
    the conclusion stands.
15. Section 6, "Under B1 the seasonal normal takes most of the attribution, as section 5 expects": the seasonal normal
    is the largest family share under B1 (fire 0.811, pm25 0.703, ndvi 0.443), but NDVI's is below one half (its
    local drivers have 0.420). Section 5 says that B1 carries the seasonal cycle; it gives no size.

Latent issues, with no effect here

16. Two latent deviations of `compare_shap.py`: item 8's top 10 is ordered by `np.argsort(-Iz)`, not by section 8's
    name tie rule (no tie occurred: the 12 largest item-8 shares are distinct for all five arcsine-link models); and
    the AW3 header lists at most 30 structural zeros (the largest count at any level is 29, defo's feature level).
17. Three latent weaknesses of `reconcile.py`. `classify()` puts a key into a group by its pattern and by what the
    official files lack, without looking at the A-against-B status (A2 shows that every key of the groups whose
    diagnosis says A = B has AB = match). `verdict()` does not count the official-derived statuses AOd and BOd as
    mismatches (all 921 derived keys are present in A and B: 916 match, and the 5 that differ are the item-8 J keys
    of group 9). And A's and B's B1 records (status, run facts, defo identity) are stored side by side in
    `work\summary.json` without being compared in code, and the A/B side of the statement that no number of items
    1-8 depends on B1 is not tested by `reconcile.py` (it rests on A's open records, B's assert in `b_compare.py` and
    the reading of their scripts, item 14). The lead compared the records in code: A's equal B's in every field for
    all five problems, and both give the defo identity as 0 and 0, as does the reconciler's own recomputation.

