### A4. The three skeptics

**Blindness and discipline: ready, three minor findings.**

1. The spec writer twice started Python without `-I -B`: a package check that printed versions and a
   ModuleNotFoundError for jsonschema. It opened no project file. The skeptic's fix: nothing to redo; in later
   rounds, use `-I -B` for one-line checks too.
2. The spec writer's `inspect_structure.py` printed, besides `per_lead.json`'s structural keys, the number of its other
   top-level keys (13 per problem), after walking the key tree in memory. No other key name or value was printed.
3. In the skeptic's words, "not a rule breach but a leak path in an allowed file": PROTOCOL.md section 14 (lines
   771-777) describes what the earlier official fire comparison wrote in its AW4 files (W undefined and the other
   cells empty; no statistic), and both A and B read that passage. Both followed brief item 7 instead and report fire
   treeroute's top 3 and own-history share, so no open choice was steered. The skeptic's fix for later rounds: keep
   descriptions of official output content out of the implementers' allowed reading, or point them to it.

It also found that A never opened `V\B` and B never opened `V\A`; that every Python start by A and B used `-I -B`;
that no compare file other than `per_lead.json` (structural keys) and `bootstrap_draws.npy` was opened before the
reconciler's first opening at 18:24:54; and, from the transcripts it read (the reconciler's calls up to 18:33), that
no SHAP script was run and that no file in SHAP outside V had been created or changed from 17:09 on. For files, the
lead's own scan at 19:30, after the round, covers the whole round: of 10,801 files (V and `smoke\bardl_fix` not
entered), none was created or changed at or after 17:09, and the newest is `RUN_COMMANDS.md` (17:05:41); a report
checker repeated the scan with the same result. That no SHAP script ran after 18:33 follows from the scan only
indirectly (such a run writes logs or outputs).

**The numbers: ready, three minor findings.**

1. NDVI's pairs with BARDL. BARDL gives exactly 0 to 93 of the 96 variables of J, so its top-5, top-10 and top-20 sets
   are filled by name order: section 8's tie rule, which all three sources applied in Python code-point ascending
   order (the spec's C10 leaves the direction and collation open). Code-point order puts `aet_total_mm` first, a
   variable BARDL does not use. The BARDL pairs' Jaccard, RBO and top-20-union Spearman therefore depend on that rule:
   - rival_bardl_h1|treeroute, variable level: Jaccard top 5 is 0.25 with `aet_total_mm` and 0.11 without it (top
     10: 0.25 against 0.18); RBO p 0.9 is 0.365, or 0.325 under another collation;
   - rival_bardl_h1|rival_rf, variable level: `aet_total_mm` is also a tie-only overlap of the top 10; the
     top-20-union Spearman is 0.162 or 0.038 and RBO p 0.95 0.241 or 0.322, by collation;
   - rival_bardl_h1|rival_lgbm, variable level: top-20-union Spearman 0.111 or 0.030;
   - rival_bardl_h1|treeroute, feature level: `x__V02_total_precip_mm_roll3`, a column BARDL does not read, is a
     tie-only overlap of the top 20 (Jaccard top 20 0.111, or 0.143 case-insensitive), and the top-20-union Spearman
     is -0.064, -0.126 or 0.003 under the three collations;
   - rival_bardl_h1|rival_ridge, feature level: top-20-union Spearman -0.471, -0.393 or -0.362.

   A, B and the official files share these numbers. The main-text rows are not affected: under three collations no
   main-row number moves by more than 3e-5. No pair without BARDL, in any problem or at either level, has a top-k
   overlap that exists only through a tie, or a collation dependence above 1e-12 (the skeptic's script s41). The
   skeptic's alternative to a footnote, not showing these cells, is not proposed (A5, point 3).
2. This report was missing (it was written at 19:31; this is its second version). The skeptic asked that every
   number the report quotes be checked against its values (`s92_final_lines.json`). The facts checker compared every
   cell of the section-3 main-row table and of the section-6 B1 tables with A's `results.json`, whose values equal
   the skeptic's, and found the four double-rounded cells of A1 item 4; every other cell agrees.
3. PM2.5's RBO against the consensus, 0.3720 [0.3645, 0.5707], lies at the low edge of its interval because its
   bootstrap distribution has two peaks. The consensus top 3 lie within 0.0035 of each other (spi 0.0813,
   sst_anom_global_tropical 0.0791, hist_last 0.0778); on draws where hist_last, treeroute's top variable, becomes the
   consensus's top variable, the RBO jumps. 91.1% of the draws lie above the full-sample value.

With its own code, written from the protocol's words, the skeptic recomputed the five main rows (fire's defined
part), a sample of pairs, coral treeroute's importances, defo's identity, and a sample of the fire, pm25 and ndvi B1
numbers (fire: Spearman over J, the top-20-union Spearman, RBO p 0.9, the share and family-share distances, the
family shares, the own-history share and the top 10; pm25 and ndvi: Spearman over J, the share and family-share
distances, the own-history share, the size of J and the top 3). Every one equals A and B, and the official files
where those exist (largest difference 1.5e-15); the B1 numbers have no official counterpart. It regenerated the five
draw matrices from section 3's generator and section 8's salt (all equal to `bootstrap_draws.npy`) and checked the
SHA-256 of all 29 row files against their records.

**The reconciliation: not ready, one major and eight minor findings.** The major finding: this report did not exist,
so none of the reconciliation's conclusions was written down and its counts could not be confirmed; the B1 checks and
the statement that no number of items 1-8 depends on B1 existed only as raw fields of `work\summary.json`, A's and B's
records there were never compared in code, and `reconcile.py` does not check the A/B side of the independence
statement. The report is written now (with the corrected counts of A1 items 5 and 6, and the second check of 3,953
table cells), and the uncompared records are answered in A1 item 17. The skeptic also asked, ideally, that the
side-by-side B1 records become asserts; `reconcile.py` is left as it ran, and the lead's comparison in code stands in
their place. Its eight minor findings are A1 items 9 to 13 and 16, and the first two parts of item 17. The skeptic
also re-ran `reconcile.py` in a copy (`V\skeptic\reconcile\rerun`): `mismatches.json` and `summary.json` came out
equal in every field but the run times, and its own mapping of every official row agreed within 1e-12 apart from
the known groups.

### A5. Points for the owner

1. **Numbers the protocol asks for but `compare_shap.py` did not write:** fire's main-row treeroute top 3 and
   own-history share [CI] (group 1), and the sign-agreement intervals (group 2). A and B computed them and agree, and
   their full-sample values equal the official ones. Section 9 lists the comparison's outputs as the files in
   `compare\` (lines 540-542), and section 10 makes S3.13 the AW3 tables (lines 553-556); only for B1 does section 10
   give the numbers to A and B (lines 605-607). Two routes:
   - (a) take them from A (= B), say so in S3.13 and record it in PROTOCOL.md section 14; S3.13 then differs from the
     `compare\` files in these cells. Proposed.
   - (b) a corrected comparison: a new script (the existing SHAP scripts are not edited) that writes fire's top 3 and
     own-history share whatever the rivals, and evaluates the sign agreement on the draws; run it, record it in
     section 14 and re-check the changed cells against A (= B). S3.13 then equals the official files, at the cost of
     a new script, its checks and a run.

   Either way, three of fire's variable-level sign-agreement intervals rest on few finite draws and are all [0, 1]:
   rival_ridge|treeroute on 64 of 2,000 (its full-sample value is undefined), rival_lgbm|treeroute on 1,140 and
   rival_rf|treeroute on 1,380. Section 8 asks for each interval with its number of finite replicates. Proposed:
   print them with that number. (The spec's C21 leaves open the fewest finite replicates for which an interval is
   given, and compare_shap made no choice there, as it evaluated no draw.)
2. **The feature-level family-share distance** (A1 item 12):
   - (a) show it at both levels, as the words of sections 8 and 10 favour: at the feature level it is the
     variable-level value repeated, with a note; compare_shap's omission then counts as a third deviation (with no
     new number), and B1's feature-level value is reported (it equals the variable-level one). Proposed.
   - (b) variable level only, with a note that the feature level would repeat it; B1 then follows compare_shap's
     choice (section 10).
3. **NDVI's BARDL pair cells** (A4, the numbers, item 1). Keep them, with a footnote: BARDL gives 0 to 93 of its 96
   variables, so its top-k sets are filled by name order (section 8's tie rule, applied in code-point ascending order;
   the spec's C10), at both levels; give the size of the effect (for example Jaccard top 5 with treeroute: 0.25, or
   0.11 without the tie-only `aet_total_mm`). Section 8 asks for every pair, and section 10 and ruling BA1 already
   state the name-order fill (lines 618-623), so not showing the cells, the skeptic's alternative, would conflict with
   both. Proposed: keep, with the footnote.
4. **Presentation:**
   - Empty families: print the defined 0 with a label, as the reconcile skeptic's fix reads: coral's own-history
     shares 0.00 [0.00, 0.00] "(no own-history input)" and defo's context-and-calendar share 0.0% "(no context or
     calendar input)". Proposed. The alternative, words in place of the number, would change section 8's row content
     and is for the owner to rule.
   - The rivals' median: one column label for every row, "median of the rivals" (for defo and pm25 it is
     rival_lgbm's share; for NDVI the mean of the two middle of four shares). Proposed. Alternative: keep section 8's
     "median rival", with a footnote for NDVI.
   - PM2.5's RBO against the consensus: a one-line note that its interval is asymmetric because the consensus's top
     variable changes across draws (A4, the numbers, item 3). Proposed.
5. **Intervals for the main models' top-10 shares and family shares** (groups 10 and 11): compare_shap writes none;
   A's (= B's) exist. S3.13 will show intervals on B1's shares, because section 10 asks for them.
   - (a) no intervals for the main models' shares, as in the official files. Proposed.
   - (b) add A's (= B's) intervals, which come from outside the official files as in point 1 (a).
6. **Two earlier questions** (asked on 2026-11-16 at about 01:50). C10, still open: mention in S3.13 that coral's
   pilot `AW4_table.md` was read on 2026-11-13 at about 15:31 (section 1 above). Proposed. B-05 no longer arises:
   defo's identity check gave exactly 0, so no B1 number is held back.

